#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060493} :activity/id #uuid "21fdb5f1-db87-5c60-b2e3-449b3c8d22a8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050710 :topic/id #uuid "6087772c-6185-54a0-92b8-45b592dbd0ad" :topic/math-academy-id 169} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060493 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060882 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a general triangle where\n\n- side $a$ is *opposite* angle $\\angle A,$\n\n- side $b$ is *opposite* angle $\\angle B,$ and\n\n- side $c$ is *opposite* angle $\\angle C,$\n\nas follows:\n\n![](images/8f/8f3d3bf4824508ef152e8d8d567672d7d61fb36e0ffb533d97be7294e9198956.png)\n\nThe **law of cosines** states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2} & ={b}^{2}+{c}^{2}-2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A \\\\ {b}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{c}^{2}-2ac\\operatorname{cos}⁡B \\\\ {c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2}-2ab\\operatorname{cos}⁡C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use this to calculate the length of one side when we know two sides and one angle.\n\nIf we look at the law of cosines we see that it's quite similar to the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\na^2+b^2=c^2\n$$\n\nBut remember that the Pythagorean theorem can *only* be used for a *right-angled triangle*!\n\n![](images/db/db9ed4aac9ca46ffc95d393d863cb09e9ba874caf0008410937f961fdf6c7ab6.png)\n\nThe law of cosines is more general and can be used on *any* triangle.\n\n**Note:** To remember the law of cosines, it's helpful to notice that the equations always follow the same pattern:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{pmatrix}\\,\\text{side opposite}\\, \\\\ \\,\\text{the angle}\\,\\end{pmatrix}}^{2}\\,=\\,{\\begin{pmatrix}\\,\\text{adjacent}\\, \\\\ \\,\\text{side}\\,\\end{pmatrix}}^{2}\\,+\\,{\\begin{pmatrix}\\,\\text{adjacent}\\, \\\\ \\,\\text{other side}\\,\\end{pmatrix}}^{2}\\,-\\,2\\begin{pmatrix}\\,\\text{adjacent}\\, \\\\ \\,\\text{side}\\,\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}\\,\\text{adjacent}\\, \\\\ \\,\\text{other side}\\,\\end{pmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\text{angle}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll show how this formula can be derived at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "bdb04bba-3835-534a-8187-8b828854f81e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2464 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4e5717a6-68f0-5158-8484-d4001a77e16f" :step/math-academy-id 11024 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060494}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060494 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059653 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059482 :question/id #uuid "0383d558-2227-5c47-b0d3-8ac48c7b1e7a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3286"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f372d875-4de9-5ed1-86a6-80ceeddd3fe7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049224}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573318}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Length of a Triangle's Side Using the Law of Cosines"} :step/id #uuid "eb62fd93-0b20-52e8-bd24-8239ba33c0bf" :step/math-academy-id 11025 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060495}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060495 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060884 :tutorial/content "Let's look at our general triangle again.\n\n![](images/8f/8f3d3bf4824508ef152e8d8d567672d7d61fb36e0ffb533d97be7294e9198956.png)\n\nWhen we know the lengths of three sides of a triangle, we rearrange the law of cosines to find an angle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2} & ={b}^{2}+{c}^{2}-2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A \\\\ 2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A & ={b}^{2}+{c}^{2}-{a}^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡A & =\\frac{{b}^{2}+{c}^{2}-{a}^{2}}{2bc}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for all the angles, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡A & =\\frac{{b}^{2}+{c}^{2}-{a}^{2}}{2bc}, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡B & =\\frac{{a}^{2}+{c}^{2}-{b}^{2}}{2ac}, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡C & =\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}-{c}^{2}}{2ab}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's helpful to notice that the equations always follow the same pattern:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\text{angle})=\\frac{{\\begin{pmatrix}\\text{adjacent} \\\\ \\text{side}\\end{pmatrix}}^{2}+{\\begin{pmatrix}\\text{adjacent} \\\\ \\text{other side}\\end{pmatrix}}^{2}-{\\begin{pmatrix}\\text{side opposite} \\\\ \\text{the angle}\\end{pmatrix}}^{2}}{2\\begin{pmatrix}\\text{adjacent} \\\\ \\text{side}\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}\\text{adjacent} \\\\ \\text{other side}\\end{pmatrix}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0bfa5e32-194d-54b1-adf5-f72b45e48fb4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2467 :tutorial/title "Using the Law of Cosines to Calculate an Unknown Angle"} :step/id #uuid "a5b6a9a3-0ce2-5918-bbe5-1f4d666fd6ba" :step/math-academy-id 11028 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060496}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060496 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059654 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059483 :question/id #uuid "84630f24-0d62-5ab3-8f9d-e03e815820cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-3288"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8da5ae4c-c94c-5a8c-ba62-5df6e500008a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049794}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573274}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Measure of an Angle in a Triangle Using the Law of Cosines"} :step/id #uuid "4158324f-f5e3-5d83-8e5e-ae54e6256c2d" :step/math-academy-id 11031 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060803}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060803 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060863 :tutorial/content "Let's now derive the law of cosines in the following form:\n\n$$\na^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2bc\\cos A\n$$\n\nConsider $\\triangle ABC$ with sides of lengths $a,b,$ and $c,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/7c/7cc2a0cbb6a02af829e070fc54cfdbe616683f86527b119a530053a34048e699.png)\n\nLet's now add the height $\\overline{CH}$ and assume it has length $h,$ as shown.\n\n![](images/d4/d4e80b2b54283ea353b687d959e9d8fc6c9303e85081c0a46b48703ee3dae7d5.png)\n\nThe remainder of this proof consists of two steps:\n\n- **Step 1**: Write down expressions for $\\cos A$ and $\\sin A.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Apply the Pythagorean theorem to $\\triangle BHC,$ and simplify.\n\nUsing elementary trigonometry, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos A = \\dfrac{AH}{b}, \\qquad \\sin A = \\dfrac{h}{b},\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\nAH =  b\\cos A, \\qquad h = b\\sin A.\n$$\n\nApplying the Pythagorean theorem to $\\triangle BHC$ and using our expression for $h,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2} & =(BH)^{2}+{h}^{2} \\\\ {a}^{2} & =(BH)^{2}+{b}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the length of the side $\\overline{AB}.$ Since $\\overline{AB}$ consists of two segments, $\\overline{AH}$ and $\\overline{BH},$ we have\n\n$$\nc = AH + BH\\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad BH = c - AH,\n$$\n\nand using our expression for $AH,$ we have\n\n$$\nBH = c - b\\cos A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our expression for $a^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2} & =(BH)^{2}+{b}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡A \\\\  & =(c-b\\operatorname{cos}⁡A)^{2}+{b}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡A \\\\  & ={c}^{2}-2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A+{b}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡A+{b}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡A \\\\  & ={b}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡A+{b}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡A+{c}^{2}-2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A \\\\  & ={b}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡A+{sin}^{2}⁡A)+{c}^{2}-2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that $\\cos^2A + \\sin^2 A = 1$ for any angle $A$ (this is the Pythagorean theorem in trigonometric form). Therefore, we have\n\n$$\na^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2bc\\cos A\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nWe can use a similar argument to prove the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}^{2} &={a}^{2}+{c}^{2}-2ac\\operatorname{cos}⁡B \\\\ {c}^{2} &={a}^{2}+{b}^{2}-2ab\\operatorname{cos}⁡C\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6ee27fb2-d873-53a9-b674-446b546b19ec" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13281 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Law of Cosines"} :step/id #uuid "c0891e57-fce7-50ae-a891-40c8707b98aa" :step/math-academy-id 461992}] :activity/title "The Law of Cosines" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059897}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573366} :activity/id #uuid "eb199040-a06f-5cc5-8c2f-a1c44f97b628" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050710 :topic/id #uuid "6087772c-6185-54a0-92b8-45b592dbd0ad" :topic/math-academy-id 169} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573337 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573318 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bba957a-99fe-5340-a47d-f10e2d2b6869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572855} {:answer/id #uuid "8be15e53-3455-5adc-9a97-477c9964ac51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12.43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573004} {:answer/id #uuid "bdb54cb1-4cdf-5e1b-86e5-3c1797fc5bf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573065} {:answer/id #uuid "e6bcf4ed-a6f1-5dad-93c0-7edac4fd7cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573118} {:answer/id #uuid "f46016ce-d644-5ff1-b8c1-c24047295c26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9.69" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573134}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573134} :answer-field/id #uuid "e61d7995-875b-5a12-bc2c-d4a1dc9575fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "edd7453d-0827-54b9-87f7-014fa66cf7ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-14506" :question/problem "A triangle has sides $j$, $k$, and $l.$ The sides $j$ and $l$ have lengths $4$ and $7$, respectively. If the angle $K$ between $j$ and $l$ has a measure of ${121}^{∘}$, what is the length of side $k?$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](images/1f/1fde3029f6a50388ebd342dc4d15e9787567461b651c24cd6bd6843521b0465d.png)\n\nThe Law of Cosines states that\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}={j}^{2}+{l}^{2}-2jl\\operatorname{cos}⁡K.\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nm∠K={121}^{∘},\\,j=4,\\,l=7.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the numbers into the formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{7}^{2}-2(4)(7)\\operatorname{cos}⁡{121}^{∘} \\\\  & =16+49-56\\operatorname{cos}⁡{121}^{∘} \\\\  & =65-56(-0.515\\,03…) \\\\  & =65+28.842\\,13 \\\\  & =93.842\\,13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places. \n\nTaking the square root gives\n\n$$\nk=\\sqrt{93.842\\,13}=9.69\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "27597e6e-46a5-5c0e-98fc-2488a4f8741d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573360 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f7d18b7-8669-51a0-8612-923a2719727c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{21.20}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572858} {:answer/id #uuid "6437eb0c-5020-559e-bd70-319aee5ba937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{17.89}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572946} {:answer/id #uuid "c646cdc4-684d-54a1-8714-3eeef1e84d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{18.19}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573074} {:answer/id #uuid "c9b37447-949a-5c58-9208-515eaa569613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{16.43}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573078} {:answer/id #uuid "d4bc5f15-b8d5-57ea-919a-885ac1bd4741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{20.36}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573095}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573074} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe46b4b1-2f2f-5fb3-9e1d-afbfc19fa446" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5021d5eb-c519-52ae-b30c-1049345fdc26" :question/math-academy-id "q-6423" :question/problem "A triangle has sides $m$, $n$, and $o.$ The sides $m$, $n$, and $o$ are of lengths  $2$, $5$, and $6$, respectively. What is the measure of the angle between the sides $n$ and $o?$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](images/26/26f2041aec84bb0086e2f08c2ba3a19331895951782b7fda153858643a8a5dd4.png)\n\nWe use the Law of Cosines to solve for angle $M:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡M & =\\frac{{o}^{2}+{n}^{2}-{m}^{2}}{2no}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHere, we have\n\n$$\nm=2,\\,n=5,\\,o=6.\n$$\n\nThis gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡M & =\\frac{{6}^{2}+{5}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{2(5)(6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{36+25-4}{60} \\\\  & =\\frac{57}{60}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠M & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{57}{60}) \\\\  & ={18.19}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "ae5ad8bd-10ef-5231-be59-86befa29f9d8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573337}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573366 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc94e018-ff1c-54a4-ad53-58d72fb46ced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40.62^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058519} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a71e64-a915-5b68-8c12-ba28c3cc8ce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "44.42^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058520} {:answer/id #uuid "1815a363-18a5-5d2d-80fe-3dc0a386322e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "39.38^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058521} {:answer/id #uuid "27214cfc-8510-5a14-b0c9-3c4177cbf56f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "41.63^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058522} {:answer/id #uuid "38dcd079-bdf0-5063-bde9-c2d72e34058b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "44.53^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058523}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8a66371c-5e30-50a2-b4c7-0a01de25c766" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0a1ec4ee-60c9-5336-ad34-95ed5dc973af" :question/math-academy-id "q-6404" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2ba81c227223d872727ceec1e6b097b69d3f4b47cd9f7190ab984c2124c1bf5.png)\n\nFor the triangle above, find $m∠A$ in degrees rounded to two decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true} :step/id #uuid "fa217fcf-e6e8-5a8b-b755-644e946d7b8b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573360}}] :activity/title "The Law of Cosines" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572825}]}
